# KNOWN GAP — BOTH LANES, an ICE. `.iter().filter_map(f)` where the closure
# returns `Option[U]` aborts the Rust lowerer before codegen: `gg check`
# accepts the program, `gg build` panics.
#
#     GIR validation errors:
#       function @Vector__Option__GorgetString__iter, bb0:
#         reference to undefined type 'VectorIter__Option__GorgetString'
#       function @Vector__Option__GorgetString__drain, bb0:
#         reference to undefined type 'VectorDrain__Option__GorgetString'
#     thread 'gg-main' panicked at src/ir/lowering/mod.rs:1724:
#       GIR module failed validation (2 errors)
#
# The closure's `Option[String]` return registers `Vector__Option__GorgetString`
# and its protocol methods, but the `VectorIter`/`VectorDrain` instances those
# methods reference are never registered — so the adapter types the equip
# methods name do not exist. Note the failure is in `Vector[Option[String]]`'s
# OWN protocol, not in `FilterMapIter`: `filter_map` is only what puts an
# `Option[String]` into a collection position in the first place.
#
# ⚠ NOT the R48 Track-γ trait-default param-binding class, and not its
# HOF-accumulator sibling. Both of those produce a wrong VALUE at rc 0; this
# one never reaches codegen at all. RE-VERIFIED on the PRE-fix compiler
# (`/tmp/gg_base`, built from the base blobs): byte-identical panic, so R48
# neither caused it nor touched it.
#
# ⚠ THE ELEMENT TYPE IS NOT THE AXIS — measured, so nobody scopes this as a
# resource defect. `Option[int]` reproduces identically, only with the names
# substituted: `reference to undefined type 'VectorIter__Option__int64_t'` /
# `'VectorDrain__Option__int64_t'`. Whatever registers `Vector__Option__U`'s
# protocol methods does not register the adapter instances they reference, for
# any `U`.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints 3 then 101.

from std.collections import Vector
from std.conv import int_to_str

void main():
    Vector[int] nums = [1, 2, 3]
    Vector[String] b = nums.iter().filter_map((int n): Some(int_to_str(n + 100))).collect()
    print(f"{b.len()}")
    print(b.get(0).unwrap())
